<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · 1605–1703 (Ekathotsarot to Phetracha)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of the Ayutthaya Kingdom</h1><p class="soc-sub">From King to Slave</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wat-chaiwatthanaram"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wat-chaiwatthanaram.jpg" alt="Wat Chaiwatthanaram"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wat-chaiwatthanaram">Wat Chaiwatthanaram</a><span class="soc-credit">Average trinmo · CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> ranked every person by law. Under the sakdina system, which King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borommatrailokkanat">Borommatrailokkanat</a> fixed in 1454 and the Three Seals Law records, each rank carried a number, in name the rai of land its holder was entitled to: 5 for a slave, 20 for a commoner, 600 for a learned monk, 10,000 for a chief minister and 100,000 for the uparaja, the king’s chosen successor. What the numbers measured in practice was manpower. Every free man was registered as a phrai under a lord, who called him up for labour and war, and an official’s standing rested on how many phrai he commanded. A service nobility of a few thousand ran the kingdom for a king revered as semi-divine. Outside the scale, to some extent, stood the Buddhist monks and the Chinese, who owed no labour service. In the 17th century, an age of peace and trade, foreigners rose high at court, and almost every succession to the throne was fought out in the capital.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> 1605–1703 (Ekathotsarot to Phetracha)</span><span><b>People</b> c. 1.5–3 million (est.)</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 7</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Phra Chao Phaendin</span> <span class="soc-en">(The king)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:39%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-est" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Princes of the royal clan</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">Fewer than 1 in 1,000 (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:54%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-est" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:1%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Mun nai</span> <span class="soc-en">(Officials and lords)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.3–1% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:70%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-est" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:2%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Sangha</span> <span class="soc-en">(Monks and court Brahmins)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 1–2% (est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-est" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:1%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Chinese and foreign communities</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.5–2% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:85%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:79%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Phrai</span> <span class="soc-en">(Commoners)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">About 79% (the rest, est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-enslaved soc-est" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:18%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">That</span> <span class="soc-en">(Enslaved people)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 10–25% (est.)</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people. Shares marked est. are estimates where no count survives; their boxes and bars are dashed.</p></section>
