<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Sources and further reading</h2><ul class="essay-refs"><li>Baker, C. and Phongpaichit, P. (2017) A History of Ayutthaya: Siam in the Early Modern World. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.</li><li>Lieberman, V. (2003) Strange Parallels: Southeast Asia in Global Context, c. 800–1830. Volume 1: Integration on the Mainland. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.</li><li>Wyatt, D. K. (2003) Thailand: A Short History. New Haven: Yale University Press.</li><li>Wood, W. A. R. (1924) A History of Siam. London: Fisher Unwin.</li><li>Marcinkowski, M. I. (2005) From Isfahan to Ayutthaya: Contacts between Iran and Siam in the 17th Century. Singapore: Pustaka Nasional.</li></ul><p class="soc-method">The shares are of the whole population, children included. Where a scholar has counted or estimated a rank, its share follows that work, named on the rank; where no count survives, the share is an estimate, marked est. and drawn dashed, made from what the rank was and did, from related figures and from the same rank in comparable societies, and its note says what it rests on. Population: Estimate: no count for the 17th century survives in the sources used; the Burmese war of 1765–1767 killed some 200,000 people and carried 30,000–100,000 off to Burma, losses that left Siam depopulated for much of the following century.</p></section>
